course: dictionary
lesson: 16177
title: Xmas, a short way to write Christmas
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Xmas is a short, informal way of writing Christmas. You will see it on cards, shop signs and in text messages, such as «Happy Xmas!» and «Xmas sale this weekend». When you read it, read it as Christmas.
explain: The X is not a random letter. It comes from the Greek letter chi, which is the first letter of the word for Christ in Greek. People have used this shortcut for many centuries, so it is an old idea, not a modern trick.
explain: Xmas is informal, so use it in cards, notes and texts to friends. In school essays, letters and other formal writing, write «Christmas» in full.
fact: Xmas is a noun, and it means Christmas.
fact: Xmas is an informal short form of Christmas.
fact: The X comes from the Greek letter chi, the first letter of Christ in Greek.
fact: Christmas is the Christian festival that celebrates the birth of Jesus, when many families give presents.
fact: In formal writing, use «Christmas» instead of «Xmas».
exercise: Which word is an informal short form of Christmas? | *Xmas | Xylophone | Xerox | Xenon
exercise: You see this on a card: «Happy Xmas, Grandma! Love from Sam.» Where would you most likely see the word Xmas? | In a maths test | In a science book about rocks | *On a Christmas card | In a formal letter to the council
exercise: Where does the X in Xmas come from? | A word meaning ten | A letter from the name Xavier | *The Greek letter chi, the first letter of Christ in Greek | A shortcut invented by modern texters
test: The shop window said «Xmas sale this weekend». What does Xmas mean here? | *Christmas | A kind of music | Half price all year | Exit
test: Which word should you use in a formal school essay? | Xm | Xmas | *Christmas | Chrimbo
exam: Why should you write Christmas in full in a formal essay? | Xmas is a Greek word, so it is not allowed | Christmas is shorter and quicker to write | *Xmas is informal, and Christmas is the full, formal word | Xmas means something different from Christmas
